	(text_box "ADM - TIMER OFF\n1s of cooldown to not heat up the part to failure. A short circuit would cause the transistor to heat up to 120°C (<150° limit) if it doesnt cool off before retrying it can burn. 1s might not even be enough for indefinetelly retrying"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 264.16 377.19 0)
		(size 63.5 20.4263)
		(margins 1.3525 1.3525 1.3525 1.3525)
		(stroke
			(width 0.8)
			(type solid)
			(color 200 200 200 1)
		)
		(fill
			(type none)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(face "Arial")
				(size 1.778 1.778)
				(color 0 0 0 1)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "02575de6-292a-4ee1-ab8d-95e9600cd7e1")
	)
	(text_box "This exists mainly to slow the turn on and limit inrush.\n\nSR (V/ms) = I_{inrush (mA)}/C_{out(uF)}\nC_{dVdt (pF)} = 4600 / SR(V/ms)\nP_{D (inrush)} = 0.5 * Vin * Iinrush -> must be allowed by Fig 8-2\nSR = 0.26 V/ms\n\nWe use the bus capactiance of the different busses to estimate the inrush and the power dissipation on the device, we find inrush using:\nI_{inrush} = C_{load} * dV/dt = 2700 * dV/dt\n\nThe maximum tolerated power dissipation allows for a certain power and a certain time. For some rails the device tolerates enough inrush toeasily manage the kind of load capacitance we have on the rover, for others it doesnt. The solutionnwas mainly to extend the ramp up times to not trigger the thermal shutdown on the device. The ramp up times specified for the different buses fo the rover:\n\n5V ->   35ms of ramp up\n		total startup time 65ms\n		CdVdT = 33nF\n\n\n15V ->  267ms of ramp up\n		total startup time 346ms (this could have sequencing issues, we will see)\n		CdVdT = 82nF\n\n24V ->	1147ms of ramp up\n		total startup time 1383 (tooo much -> use better ADM1270)\n		CdVdT = 220nF\n	"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 274.32 177.8 0)
		(size 66.04 70.8366)
		(margins 1.3525 1.3525 1.3525 1.3525)
		(stroke
			(width 0.8)
			(type solid)
			(color 200 200 200 1)
		)
		(fill
			(type none)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(face "Arial")
				(size 1.27 1.27)
				(color 0 0 0 1)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "174933a7-0a9e-48f2-b631-3bf4a54b06a2")
	)
	(text_box "DESIGN - Cout\nUsing TPS:\n\n5000uF for 5V rail\n3500uF for 15V rail\n\nUsing ADM1270:\n\n3500uF for 24V rail"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 472.44 323.85 0)
		(size 40.64 45.09)
		(margins 1.3525 1.3525 1.3525 1.3525)
		(stroke
			(width 0.8)
			(type solid)
			(color 200 200 200 1)
		)
		(fill
			(type none)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(face "Arial")
				(size 2.54 2.54)
				(color 0 0 0 1)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "175f63cc-1c49-4735-a983-e41100ae2022")
	)
	(text_box "ADM - CHANGELOG\nADM was first removed in a rerouting done originally on the v2 but due to the limitations of the TPS package it was readded to be tested if the TPS 24V fails. With the selected transistors from v1 it can potentially allow for more than the TPS although derating the transistors puts it in the limit of thermal damage for the delivered currents. As the only implementation where the TPS fails its the 24V rail then we can limit the current limit for 24V to 6A which passes derating. By changing the transistor it potentially increases thermal limit to 10A for 24V (and 15V if we required it)\nBy how does the ADM work inrush doesnt change the current (as that is going to be ILIM for startup) but adds extra stress with ramp up time. By sizing the transistor with extra margin on the SOA plot it can support a bigger Cout than the TPS"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 402.59 275.59 0)
		(size 157.48 45.09)
		(margins 1.3525 1.3525 1.3525 1.3525)
		(stroke
			(width 0.8)
			(type solid)
			(color 200 200 200 1)
		)
		(fill
			(type none)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(face "Arial")
				(size 2.54 2.54)
				(color 0 0 0 1)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "28aeca59-daa4-46cf-9544-530e6b929190")
	)
	(text_box "TPS - TRACO\n\nIn order to resize this components pwoer sequencing was taken into account. TRACO regulators already have startup inrush current limiter so these protections became partly redundant (until those other protections are propertly tested they are not). As we are still keeping this protections to limit the potential harm to the TRACOS the already present current limit of the TRACOS needs to be taken into account. If, due to the values of UV of this device, the two ramp ups did not overlap then this would be an issue. In order for this to happen we woudl need UV at 90% of VOUT so when the TRACO output arrives at this value at the end of its ramp up the TPS could begin. As this would put the UV values too close to the actual bus this was not implemented. Instead both ramps are present at the same time. From the startup time (75ms typical) and the supported Couts from TRACOs datasheet the allowed currents were deduced:\n24V 3700uF TEP200 1A\n15V 6300uF TEP160 1.18A\n5V 40000uF TEP100 2.5A\n\nAs I said TRACO already has a I limit at 120-150% of output Imax so the current limit should be put around 110% to not damage the TRACO but still allow for max current to be used\nFor the 5V one it wont be possible because TRACO allows 20A but TPS only 18A max "
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 402.59 189.23 0)
		(size 157.48 83.82)
		(margins 1.3525 1.3525 1.3525 1.3525)
		(stroke
			(width 0.8)
			(type solid)
			(color 200 200 200 1)
		)
		(fill
			(type none)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(face "Arial")
				(size 2.54 2.54)
				(color 0 0 0 1)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "3264763f-0c56-4d9c-ad5e-f5da3f3c5f1f")
	)
	(text_box "Taken from table in datasheet, 220pF @ 9.3ms retry delay for 5V and 15V, 2.2nF @ 100ms for 24V (if it works, its going to be thermally tight, needs to cool off during retries)"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 191.77 128.27 0)
		(size 67.31 8.9622)
		(margins 1.3525 1.3525 1.3525 1.3525)
		(stroke
			(width 0.8)
			(type solid)
			(color 200 200 200 1)
		)
		(fill
