		(uuid "91ed200b-3f8b-42b0-a71b-aee17af76f98")
	)
	(text_box "TPS - CHANGELOG\n\nAfter v1 of the TPS implementation we had:\n- 5V working well\n- 15V working limited: passed through startup fine with the exact system, anything more on the bus and the inrush current was too much to start.\n- 24V not working at all: failed to startup (worked on steady state on lab bench, inrush too much to handle).\n\nAfter redoing the design to prepare for v2 some things were clarified:\nTPS has a Thermal Shutdown to protect the die of a overtemperature event (aka to much power dissipated because of the power passing through). To size the component TI gives a series of graphs to that map the allowed ramp up time for a given power dissipation to not trigger this thermal shutdown. When applying the ramp times from v1 (19ms @ 5V, 58ms @ 15V and 92ms @24V) nominally 5V and 15V were correctly sized according to the datasheets Figure 8-2. For the 24V the dissipated power was already too much for this part (8.4W). For the 15V althopugh it passed nominally when adding tolerances to the design it failed. Thats because at the 58ms the power dissipation allowed by the device (5.5W) was just over the actual power dissipated (5W). This explain the previously observed behaviour. Also all this is on the premise that theres no DC load on the bus at startup that could add even more power dissipation onto the device.\n power dissipated on the 24V was too much at the specified 90 ms of voltage ramp time.\n\nThis issues and the fact that the newly received DC/DC of TRACO systems could give more power than the one used to size this protections motivated the redesign."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 402.59 81.28 0)
		(size 157.48 104.8308)
		(margins 1.3525 1.3525 1.3525 1.3525)
		(stroke
			(width 0.8)
			(type solid)
			(color 200 200 200 1)
		)
		(fill
			(type none)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(face "Arial")
				(size 2.54 2.54)
				(color 0 0 0 1)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "9fa473e7-3575-4b45-befe-c16adc2ac043")
	)
	(text_box "Indicator to solder after assembly"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
